Herein we report an elaborate study on the influence of the concentration of the bulky \(\mathrm{bim}^{-}\) linker on the melting temperature and glass forming behaviour of ZIF-62(M) materials. The thermal phase behaviour, physical properties and thermodynamics of melting of an extended series of eleven ZIF-62(M)- \(\mathrm{bim}_{x}\) derivatives with varying \(\mathrm{im}^{-}\mathrm{bim}^{-}\) ratios (general chemical composition \(\mathrm{M}(\mathrm{im})_{2-x}(\mathrm{bim})_{x};\mathrm{M}^{2+}=\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\) or \(\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}, x=0.02-0.35\)) are thoroughly analysed with single crystal X-ray diffraction (SCXRD), (variable temperature) X-ray powder diffraction (XRPD), X-ray total scattering (pair distribution function analysis), IR and NMR spectroscopy, simultaneous thermogravimetric analysis / differential scanning calorimetry (TGA/DSC) as well as various gas physisorption measurements. By covering the entire range from regular ZIF-4 \(\left(x=0\right)\) over the zone with low \(\mathrm{bim}^{-}\) concentration \((0.02<x<0.24)\) to conventional ZIF-62 \(\left(x\geq0.25\right)\), we unveil a subtle transition from derivatives showing sequential amorphisation/recrystallisation \(\left(x<0.05\right)\) to derivatives exhibiting coherent low temperature melting \(\left(x\geq0.05\right)\). Via adjusting \(x\), the melting point of ZIF-62 can be lowered to only \(\sim370\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), which marks by far the lowest melting point of a three-dimensional porous MOF discovered until now. Moreover, we demonstrate for the first time that the intrinsically microporous ZIF glasses can adsorb gaseous \(C_{3}\) and \(C_{4}\) hydrocarbons (i.e. propane, propylene and \(n\)-

butane). Surprisingly and despite their random network structure, thermodynamic and kinetic sorption data both indicate a preference for the adsorption of propylene over propane in the ZIF glasses, demonstrating the potential of these metal-organic glasses in propylene/propane separation (e.g. as monolithic glass membranes).

# EXPERIMENTAL SECTION

Synthesis ZIF-4(M) and ZIF-62(M)- \(\mathrm{bim}_{x}\) were prepared by adapting published protocols from the literature.17,35,40-42 \(\mathrm{M}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) (4.0 mmol, 1.2 g) and varying ratios of imidazole and benzimidazole (total molar amount 13.2 mmol, molar ratios are given in Table 1 and S1) were dissolved in N,N-dimethylformamide (DMF) to yield a total volume of 90 mL. The obtained solution was divided in ten 9 mL portions, which were transferred into ten 12 mL horosilicate vials. The deeply sealed reaction vials were transferred to a preheated oven (100 °C for the Zn-based compounds, 130 °C for the Co-based compounds) for 7 d. After cooling to room temperature, the reaction volumes were recombined, filtered and washed three times with 20 mL DMF. The ZIFs were obtained as large, truncated octahedrally shaped single crystals. After full characterization the as-synthesised materials were dried at 200 °C under dynamic vacuum \((p\sim10^{-4}\,\mathrm{kPa})\) for 20 h to obtain the evacuated materials in the form of single crystals.

