Importantly, these findings are in accordance with the observation stated earlier in this paper that the ZIF-4(M) materials amorphize at temperatures as low as \(320{-}330\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C},\) while the ZIF-62(M)- \(\cdot\mathrm{bim}_{x}\) compounds (with \(x\,\geq\,0.05\) ) melt at higher temperatures between \(370\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and \(440\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.\) . The thermal amorphization of ZIF-4(M) generates a metastable disordered material, which recrystallizes to the thermodynamically stable ZIF-zni(M) upon further heating. The ZIF\(\mathrm{zni}(\mathrm{M})\) phase itself melts (for \(\mathbf{M}^{2+}=\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}^{2+}\) ) or decomposes (for \(\mathbf{M}^{2+}=\) \(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\) ) at even higher temperatures between 550 and \(590\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) (Fig. S36- 37).17,33 In agreement with our DSC data, the variable temperature SCXRD results suggest that bond dissociation (i.e. melting or amorphisation) occurs at a higher temperature for ZIF-62(M)- \(\cdot\mathrm{bim}_{x}\) compared to conventional ZIF-4(M). As shown above, incorporation of the bulkier \(\mathrm{bim^{-}}\) linker in the framework efficiently suppresses the recrystallisation to the ZIF-zni(M) phase if \(x\,\geq\,0.05\) . Instead, the frameworks melt coherently and form ZIF liquids which are thermodynamically stable up to their decomposition temperature \((510{-}600\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\) . Thus, via fine tuning \(x\) the melting point of ZIF-62(M)- \(\cdot\mathrm{bim}_{x}\) can be drastically lowered and, at the same time, the thermal stability window of the liquid ZIF phase decisively increased. It is important that the formation of a stable ZIF liquid which does not recrystallize to ZIF-zni is already observed for very low \(\mathrm{bim^{-}}\) concentrations (i.e. about 1 \(\mathrm{bim^{-}}\) linker on \(39\,\mathrm{im}^{-}\) linkers, \(x\!=\!0.05\) ), far away from the conventional \(\mathrm{bim^{-}}\) concentration of the ZIF-62(M) materials (i.e. 1 \(\mathrm{bim^{-}}\) linker on \(7\,\mathrm{im}^{-}\) linkers, \(x\!=\!0.25\) ).

It is remarkable that so far (and including this work) melting has only been reported for ZIFs which contain the small \(\mathrm{im}^{-}\) linker. The well-studied ZIF-7, including only the bulky \(\mathrm{bim^{-}}\) linker (chemical composition \(Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{bim})_{2}\) ) does not melt.53 The same is true for ZIF-8 \((\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{mim})_{2},\mathrm{mim}^{-}\!=\!2\) -methylimidazolate) whose \(\mathrm{mim}^{-}\) linkers are only slightly larger than \(\mathrm{im}^{-}\) .14 Hence, the incorporation of \(\mathrm{im}^{-}\) , seems mandatory to achieve meltable ZIFs. Larger imidazolate-type linkers result in larger tetrahedral units enhancing steric hindrance and hampering melting. However, ZIFs featuring only the small \(\mathrm{im}^{-}\) (such as ZIF-4), do not form stable ZIF liquids at low temperature. ZIF

\(4(Z\mathfrak{n})\) as well as its polymorphs (e.g. ZIF-zeg, ZIF-nog) amorphize at \(320{-}330\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and subsequently recrystallize to ZIF-zni \((Z\mathfrak{n})\) .37 As outlined, ZIF-zni(M) is highly stable and does not melt before \({\sim}590\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) .

Nevertheless, on the basis of our experimental data we hypothesize that a great number of other ZIFs may melt at lower temperatures (i.e. much below \(590\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) ), form stable liquid phases (i.e. do not recrystallize to a denser phase) and transform into porous glasses upon cooling from the melt, as long as the following two requirements are fulfilled:

(i) The ZIF must contain \(\mathrm{im}^{-}\) linkers and at least one other imidazolate-type linker that is bulky enough to prevent recrystallisation to the ZIF-zni phase (as in the current case of ZIF-62(M), the concentration of the bulky imidazolate linker may be remarkably low, however). (ii) Naturally, the bulky imidazolate-type linker must feature a decomposition temperature higher than the framework's melting temperature in order to allow melting before decomposition.

The fact that amorphization (i.e. bond breaking and framework collapse) of ZIF-4(M) occurs at \(320{-}330\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) further suggests, that the lowest melting point that is achievable for ZIFs at atmospheric pressure lies around this region. As shown above, incorporation of just small amounts of the bulky \(\mathrm{bim^{-}}\) linker results in a melting point of \({\sim}370\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C};\) about \(40{-}50\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) higher than the temperature of amorphization of ZIF4(M). Incorporation of functionalized imidazolate linkers that are smaller than \(\mathrm{bim^{-}}\) , might however lead to the discovery of ZIFs with melting points approaching \(320{-}330\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) .

Importantly, the two requirements outlined above are already met by a number of known ZIF compounds. Melting and glass formation has previously been demonstrated for TIF-4 \((\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{im})_{1.5}(5\mathrm{-mbim})_{0.5},\) \(T_{\mathrm{m}}=\) \(467\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C},\) 5-mbim \(=5\) -methylbenzimidazolate), ZIF-76 \((Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{im})_{1.62}(5-\) \(\mathrm{Clbim})_{0.38},\) \(T_{\mathrm{m}}\,=\,451\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C},\) 5-Clbim \(=\) 5-chlorobenzimidazolate) and ZIF-76-mbim \((Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{im})_{1.33}(5\mathrm{-mbim})_{0.67},\) \(T_{\mathrm{m}}\,{=}\,471\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\) – strikingly, all of these frameworks fulfill the described requirements.13,22 Many other mixed-linker ZIFs satisfying at least the first requirement are known: ZIF-60, -70, -303, -360, -365, -376, -386, -486, -412, -413, -414 and - 725.46, 54 Future studies will show if these compounds indeed melt at elevated temperature. In analogy to what is presented here for the prototypical ZIF-62 system, it is very likely that \(T_{\mathrm{m}}\) of all of the mixedlinker ZIFs mentioned above can also be tuned systematically by adjusting the molar ratio of their two linkers.

# Gas Sorption Properties

Until today, gas sorption properties have only been reported for very few ZIF glasses.17, 22, 55 These investigations just included gases such as \(\mathrm{H}_{2},\,\mathrm{N}_{2},\) \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) so far. Here, we performed elaborative gas sorption studies on three \(\mathrm{a}_{8}\mathrm{ZIF}{-}62(Z\mathrm{n}){-}\mathrm{bim}_{x}\) representatives using \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{CO}_{2},\) as well as larger hydrocarbon gases, namely propane, propylene and \(n\) -butane. The selected a \(\mathrm{ZIF-62(Zn)-bim}_{x}\) derivatives cover a wide range of compositions \(\left(x=0.35,0.17,0.05\right)\) . We chose \(x\) \(=~0.05\) as the smallest \(x\) value, because this is the lowest \(\mathrm{bim^{-}}\) concentration allowing for coherent low temperature ZIF melting without recrystallisation to ZIF-zni \((Z\mathfrak{n})\) from the melt.
