With the aim to get further insights into the \(C_{3}\) hydrocarbon sorption properties of the ZIF glasses, time-dependent sorption profiles for propane and propylene at \(293\,\mathrm{K}\) were recorded after applying a gas pressure of \(\sim55\,\mathrm{kPa}\) (Fig. 8, S93-S97). From the uptake-time profiles it is clearly evident that the diffusion of propylene into the glasses is much faster than the diffusion of propane. In the case of agZIF-62(Zn)-bim0.05 it takes almost five times longer for propane than propylene to reach \(90\%\) of its equilibrium uptake and for agZIF-62(Zn)-bim0.17 it takes more than six times longer for propane than propylene to reach this uptake.

Furthermore, it is apparent that the diffusion rates of both \(C_{3}\) hydrocarbons strongly depend on \(x\). Due to the larger concentration of bulky \(\mathrm{bim}^{-}\)linkers and hence narrower pores, agZIF-62(Zn)-bim0.35 exhibits a much slower kinetic sorption profile compared to the glasses with lower \(\mathrm{bim}^{-}\)concentrations. Propylene takes about three times longer to reach \(90\%\) of its equilibrium uptake if \(x=0.35\) compared to \(x=0.17\) or 0.05. For propane the differences in diffusion rates are much less pronounced across the series of ZIF glasses. This observation implies that the diffusion rates and thus the kinetic sorption selectivity of particular gases of interest (e.g. other hydrocarbons) could be optimized by adjusting the linker ratios in these nanoporous ZIF glasses.

Even though current crystalline benchmark MOFs feature much higher propylene/propane selectivities (\(S_{\mathrm{IAST}}\) up to \(\sim60\))\(^{59-64}\) than the ZIF glasses investigated here, these first hydrocarbon sorption data for ZIF glasses are very encouraging for a future development of microporous MOF glasses as well as their blends and composites\(^{48,55}\) towards improved sorption properties and a higher gas separation power. The huge advantage of porous MOF glasses compared to their crystalline parent frameworks lies on the one hand in their very high thermal decomposition temperatures and on the other hand in their liquid phase processability\(^{23}\). Recent work by S. Li et al. showed that bubble-free monolithic bulk samples (i.e. pellets) of ZIF-62(Zn) can be prepared via a melting/remelting technique.\(^{65}\) The absence of grain boundaries and interfaces in such bulk MOF glasses is deemed to facilitate mass transport and gas permeability. In future work, we envisage the preparation of grain-boundary-free ZIF glass membranes of variable shapes, exhibiting gas separation properties competitive to current MOF-film-on-macroporous-support or mixed-matrix technologies.\(^{66-68}\)

# CONCLUSION

We have synthesised a series of eleven different ZIF-62(M)-bim0 metal-organic frameworks composed of different metal ions (\(\mathbf{M}^{2+}=\mathbf{C}0^{2+}\) or \(Z\mathfrak{n}^{2+}\)) and varying ratios of two different imidazolate-type linkers (the small im and the bulkier \(\mathrm{bim}^{-}\)). Investigations towards their thermal behaviour revealed that these mixed-linker ZIF materials of the general chemical composition \(\mathrm{M}(\mathrm{im})_{2-x}(\mathrm{bim})_{x}\) melt at elevated temperatures and form ZIF glasses by cooling the liquid ZIFs down to ambient temperature. It was found that the metal ion (\(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\) vs \(Z\mathfrak{n}^{2+}\)) has a major impact on the decomposition temperature (\(\sim510{-}550\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for the Co-ZIFs, \(\sim550{-}600\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for the \(Z\mathfrak{n}^{2}\) ZIFs) but only minor on the frameworks' melting point. The melting point of the ZIF compounds, however, can be engineered over a wide range between \(440\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and \(370\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) by simply adjusting the molar ratio of \(\mathrm{im}^{-}\)to \(\mathrm{bim}^{-}\)in the solid state. Remarkably, the melting point of the ZIFs is inversely proportional to the concentration of \(\mathrm{bim}^{-}\)in the material, resulting in a melting point of only \(370\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(x=0.03\) – a record low for the melting temperature of a 3D porous MOFs. ZIFs containing low concentrations of \(\mathrm{bim}^{-}\) (\(x<0.05\)) form a metastable liquid phase that partially recrystallizes to the denser ZIF-zni at about \(500\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). On the contrary, ZIFs containing higher concentrations of \(\mathrm{bim}^{-}\) (\(x\geq0.05\)) form thermodynamically stable liquids which do not recrystallize to the ZIF-zni phase.

Even though only a few ZIFs containing the small \(\mathrm{im}^{-}\)linker have been shown to melt so far, our data clearly demonstrate the impact of the bulkier \(\mathrm{bim}^{-}\)linker on the frameworks' melting point as well as on the formation of thermodynamically stable ZIF liquids. We hypothesize that a large number of literature-known crystalline ZIFs also melt as far as they are composed of \(\mathrm{im}^{-}\)linkers and at least one other bulkier imidazolate-type linker. This sets the stage for the development of a large number of new functional and chemically diverse ZIF glasses. Moreover, the low melting point of the ZIFs with \(x\sim0.05{-}0.10\) further opens the door for the lower temperature preparation of MOF glass composites\(^{20,48,65}\) incorporating temperature-sensitive compounds (e.g. inorganic complexes, organic fluorophores, etc.).

Importantly, we also prove for the first time that the glasses of the ZIF-62(M) family are not only porous towards small gas molecules such as \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\), but also towards larger \(C_{3}\) and \(C_{4}\) hydrocarbons. Adsorption isotherms and time-dependent sorption profiles indicate that the ZIF glasses are an interesting new class of materials for the separation of propylene from propane. Together with the advantages of ZIF glasses in terms of processability and shaping\(^{20,65}\) as well as their very high thermal decomposition temperatures, our findings pave the
